{
  "_comment": "The route limit covers the pre-join entrance (#712); SpaceCall loads the admitted call after Join (#730), and builds its join client from the generated ChalkPublicApi (only the unauthenticated /v1/public operations), so the MCP OAuth contract (#814) stays out of the route. The route measures about 205.5 KiB and its ceiling is 217 KiB, down from 219. Five fresh-context bundle-only runs per revision on Linux x64, 8-core Intel Xeon, Node 24.19.0, pnpm 12.8.1 and Chromium 151.0.7922.34 give non-board JavaScript gzip min/median/max of 386.486/386.486/386.486 KiB on master 35a9d072c and 386.560/386.560/386.560 KiB with send recovery. Validating the call path with Effect Schema instead of zod, which also removes zod and its English locale from the call JavaScript, cut 2.1 KiB (386.5 to 384.5 KiB on the same machine), so the non-board ceiling went down from 387 to 385 KiB. Board, CSS, timing, video-quality and leak limits and the historical timing baseline remain unchanged. The route limit alone falls, from 219 to 217 KiB. The route CSS ceiling is 28 KiB: it rose from 32 to 33 for the transcript language picker (#880) and fell to 28 in #919. Moving the SDK and UI styles onto the design-token scale (#875) turns values such as 12px into var(--space-3) inside the SDK CSS-in-JS strings, which adds 0.4 KiB gzip (385.4 KiB measured by the CI call-performance lane against 385), so the non-board ceiling goes from 385 to 386 KiB. CSS stays at 49.3 of 54 KiB. Setting the entries back to 385 undoes it. The action.* limits moved here unchanged from the latency kit's own budget file. Proposed from 40 kept cycles per plane on staging: one headless and one headed Chromium run from a 4 vCPU droplet in DigitalOcean sgp1 on 7 Nov 2026. Each p95Ms is 1.5 times the larger of the two runs' p95, rounded up to 50 ms. A limit that differs by media plane is keyed by the plane first, for example chalk_sfu.action.leave_ms; the cf_sfu ones warn until seven nightly values of that plane, from at least three nights, stay within tolerancePct. The SFU loopback and impairment tests are deterministic, so their pass rates must stay at 1 and gate from the start. proof.leave_median_ms starts as a warning at the Leave proof's own 5000 ms ceiling on the slowest leave. Real-node budgets (sfu.*, nightly action.*, proof.*, device.*) set gate to true only in a budgets pull request that cites seven nightly values, from at least three nights, with a run-to-run spread below tolerancePct. The one exception is the 18 chalk_sfu.action.* entries, which gate from 8 Nov 2026 so the own SFU has a blocking latency budget before nightly values exist: each ceiling is twice the larger p95 of the two 40-cycle runs on 7 Nov 2026 (staging, headless and headed Chromium 151, DigitalOcean sgp1 4 vCPU client, the table in #889), rounded up to 50 ms. They are loose on purpose: the 7-night rule later tightens each one to its real budget in its own pull request. New warn-only entries on 8 Nov 2026: sfu.loss_pct 0.02 is twice the worst per-run p95 loss (0.009%) of the five stall-free runs in the 7 Nov rollout data (smoke, two ramps, the 12 minute 5-class production ramp, the 1 hour 4-class soak); sfu.stalls_per_class_hour 0 because all five had none and the capacity rule is no stall over 500 ms; sfu.admission_refusals 4 is twice the worst of those runs (2, the soak). Each live run gives one value of the last two, so n is 1. proof.join_startup.* is 30000, the SDK's 30 s web startup limit, since a join over it fails instead of going live; the only measurements are the one-off runs in #526 (18.3 s for one silent dial, 11.4 to 23.3 s for 30 mixed-loss joins). No workflow scores the join proof yet. proof.leave_median_ms stays at 5000 and warn-only: the Leave proof takes five samples per run and no run values are kept. On 9 Nov 2026 chalk_sfu.action.camera_toggle.off_ms rose from 650 to 750 and chalk_sfu.action.unpublish.removed_ms from 1000 to 1100. They are interim regression ceilings measured from US-hosted runners (about 200 ms to the Singapore node): p50 636 and 947 against ceilings of 650 and 1000 made the gate depend on the runner's Azure region. They are not the accepted targets, which are much tighter, and the RTT-normalized gate replaces them. On 9 Nov 2026 the web JavaScript gzip ceilings (the 14 web.*.js_gzip_kib entries) rose from 386 to 387 KiB because the receiver's early pull from the Sync media item (#1042) adds 0.16 KiB to a call that was at 386.0; setting them back to 386 undoes it. Later on 10 Nov 2026 the same 14 entries rose from 387 to 388 KiB because the SDK code that lets the own SFU mute a camera or microphone in place (#1043) adds 0.44 KiB to a call that measured 387.4 against a master near 387.0, even while the capability is off; setting them back to 387 undoes it. Later on 10 Nov 2026 the same 14 entries rose from 388 to 389 KiB because the three pull requests that merge together each add code to the call: the own SFU mute in place (#1043, already in master), the receiver socket reuse (#1064) and the gated direct path to Sync (#1066). Five fresh-context bundle-only runs on a 4 vCPU AMD EPYC Linux x64 machine (Instabox), Node 24.21.0, pnpm 12.8.1 and Chromium 151.0.7922.34 give non-board JavaScript gzip min/median/max of 387.175/387.175/387.175 KiB on master 0ea7ed487 and 388.239/388.239/388.239 KiB on a throwaway merge of that master with #1064 (159d09a58) and #1066 (bd54bd99b), so the two add 1.064 KiB. The runs have no spread because the bytes come from one build: the first run builds and the other four reuse it. The ceiling keeps 0.76 KiB of room over the combined size. The board ceilings stay at 743 KiB against 736.667 KiB measured. Setting the 14 entries back to 388 undoes it. Retain the camera capture/picker integration allowance of 1 KiB above the master ceiling of 389 KiB: the 14 non-board JavaScript entries use 390 KiB. Historical five fresh-context bundle-only runs per revision on the same environment gave non-board JavaScript gzip min/median/max of 384.525/384.525/384.525 KiB on master 16ab8a71d and 385.493/385.493/385.493 KiB on camera revision 548fcd65c, a 0.25% integration cost; presets and segmentation remain lazy. Later on 10 Nov 2026 the same 14 entries rose from 390 to 391 KiB because the SDK safety valve that replaces the media connection before its offer reaches the media node's 64 KiB cap (#1098) adds 0.5 KiB to the call. Five fresh-context bundle-only runs on a 4 vCPU Instabox Linux x64 machine give non-board JavaScript gzip of 389.9 KiB on master 039c3b1 and 390.4 KiB on #1098 (b3b6498), the same environment, with no spread because the bytes come from one build; setting the 14 entries back to 390 undoes it. On 10 Nov 2026 the same 14 entries rose from 391 to 392 KiB because the direct signalling door (#1117: the grant door for SFU signalling and Sync, the Tunnel fallback and the path records) adds 0.6 KiB to the call. One build per revision on a 4 vCPU Instabox Linux x64 machine gives non-board JavaScript gzip of 390.9 KiB on master ff8e59a and 391.5 KiB on #1117 (6160197), the same environment, with no spread because the bytes come from one build; no cheap cut exists, since the code is the fallback logic itself and is not lazy-loadable without delaying the first join. Setting the 14 entries back to 391 undoes it. On 10 Nov 2026 the two board JavaScript entries (web.laptop.board.js_gzip_kib and web.phone.board.js_gzip_kib) rose from 743 to 744 KiB because the whiteboard toolbar (#1134) loads the board as its own chunk on first use. That takes the non-board call JavaScript from 391.5 to 374.4 KiB, and the board scenario pays for a second gzip stream plus the toolbar code itself. One build per revision on a 4 vCPU Instabox Linux x64 machine gives board JavaScript gzip of 740.0 KiB on master and 743.5 KiB on #1134, the same environment, with no spread because the bytes come from one build. Trimming the toolbar code and lazy-loading its clear dialog did not bring the board under 743: the dialog chunk split React out of the call bundle and made the board scenario 745.3 KiB. Setting the two entries back to 743 undoes it. Later on 10 Nov 2026 the same 14 entries rose from 392 to 394 KiB because the call dock polish (the tracing loader on the mic and camera icons, the hand-aware reactions button and the per-emoji tray motion) adds SDK styles and glyphs to the call: the CI call-performance lane measured 391.5 KiB for the branch on top of a master that gated at 391, and master has since grown by 0.6 KiB (#1117), so the merged call is expected near 392.5 KiB. A Mac mini bundle-only run (Node 24) gave 390.7 KiB before that merge. Setting the 14 entries back to 392 undoes it. Later on 11 Nov 2026 the two board JavaScript entries rose from 744 to 746 KiB because the call dock polish (#1110) adds 2.0 KiB to the call chunk that the board scenario also loads: the CI call-performance lane measured 745.5 KiB board JavaScript for the branch on top of master with the lazy whiteboard toolbar (#1134), against 743.5 there. Setting the two entries back to 744 undoes it. Later on 11 Nov 2026 the two board JavaScript entries rose from 746 to 751 KiB because the call connection states (#1160: the connection status card, the held last frame behind it and the guest link quality) add to the call chunk that the board scenario also loads: the CI call-performance lane measured 750.1 KiB board JavaScript and 380.9 KiB non-board JavaScript for the branch on top of master with the dock polish (#1110), the call menu (#1111) and the device access card (#1113). The non-board ceiling of 394 KiB holds. Setting the two entries back to 746 undoes it. Later on 11 Nov 2026 the route CSS ceiling rose from 28 to 28.5 KiB and the two board JavaScript entries from 751 to 754 KiB because the phone bottom sheets (#1168) put the shared sheet shell, the drag-to-dismiss logic and hold-to-open on every call: the CI call-performance lane measured 28.1 KiB of route CSS for that branch against 27.9 on master, and 753.7 KiB of board JavaScript against 750.1 (non-board JavaScript 384.5 against 380.9), after the reactions sheet, the device sheet and their CSS were already moved into lazy chunks. The 14 non-board JavaScript entries stay at 394 KiB, which holds against the lazy whiteboard (#1134). Setting the route CSS back to 28 and the board entries back to 751 undoes it. All other limits, run settings and gate flags are unchanged.",
